From: SEANCE / SoundShop2
Subject: WASAPI: recognise plain WAVE_FORMAT_IEEE_FLOAT (0x0003) capture format

Stock JUCE's WASAPIDeviceBase::tryInitialisingWithBufferSize only classifies a
stream as float when the negotiated format is WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE *and* its
SubFormat is KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_IEEE_FLOAT. Some USB capture devices (notably
the Logitech C615 HD webcam mic, and other USB-audio-class mics) report their
Windows shared-mode mix format with the *plain* WAVE_FORMAT_IEEE_FLOAT tag
(0x0003) instead of the EXTENSIBLE wrapper. JUCE then treats the stream as
integer PCM and decodes genuine 32-bit float samples through the Int32
converter, which corrupts every sample identically -> the mic records/monitors
as square-wave garbage (and a frozen capture replays equally garbled). The same
device captures cleanly under DirectSound because that is a different code path.

Fix: accept the plain WAVE_FORMAT_IEEE_FLOAT tag as float too. This only *adds*
a correct float case; any format that already worked (extensible-float, or
genuine integer PCM) is unaffected, so it cannot regress other devices.

Target: JUCE 8.0.12 (modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_WASAPI_windows.cpp).
Apply from the JUCE root, e.g.:  patch -p1 < juce-wasapi-ieee-float-tag.diff
(GNU patch tolerates line-number drift via context; for git use `git apply`.)

--- a/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_WASAPI_windows.cpp
+++ b/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_WASAPI_windows.cpp
@@ -915,9 +915,18 @@
         if (! isInitialised)
             return false;

         actualNumChannels  = format->Format.nChannels;
-        const bool isFloat = format->Format.wFormatTag == WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE && format->SubFormat == KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_IEEE_FLOAT;
+        // SEANCE patch (juce-wasapi-ieee-float-tag): also recognise the plain
+        // WAVE_FORMAT_IEEE_FLOAT (0x0003) tag, not only WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE +
+        // IEEE_FLOAT subformat. Some USB webcam / USB mics (e.g. Logitech C615)
+        // report their shared-mode mix format with the plain float tag; stock
+        // JUCE then misclassifies the stream as integer and reads genuine 32-bit
+        // float samples through the Int32 converter, producing systematic
+        // square-wave corruption on capture.
+        const bool isFloat = (format->Format.wFormatTag == WAVE_FORMAT_IEEE_FLOAT)
+                          || (format->Format.wFormatTag == WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE
+                              && format->SubFormat == KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_IEEE_FLOAT);
         bytesPerSample     = format->Format.wBitsPerSample / 8;
         bytesPerFrame      = format->Format.nBlockAlign;

         updateFormat (isFloat);
